<title>PORTUGAL: Wage cap vital for Portugal EMU entry - Finmin.</title>
<headline>Wage cap vital for Portugal EMU entry - Finmin.</headline>
<dateline>LISBON 1996-12-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Public sector wage rises next year will have to be limited so that inflation eases and Portugal can make the grade for Economic and Monetary Union (EMU), Finance Minister Antonio Sousa Franco said on Monday.</p>
<p>&quot;Obviously, the government cannot accept the risk of failing to get into EMU because it failed to control inflation,&quot; he told Reuters.</p>
<p>The government and public sector unions resumed wage talks on Monday with the two sides wide apart.</p>
<p>The government raised its offer to 2.75 percent, from 2.6 percent previously, but unions are demanding increases above 3.5 percent for next year.</p>
<p>&quot;At this moment, wage moderation is vital to inflation control,&quot; Sousa Franco said.</p>
<p>--Sergio Goncalves, Lisbon bureau 3511-3150035</p>
</text>
